"Dragontamer" <prtiglao@gmail.com> writes:
>
> I get an early wish for SDSM.

For future reference, it's probably better to use an early wish,
especially a really early wish, for the "other" DSM unless you already
have magic resistance (not the unfortunately similar-sounding magic
cancellation, which is different---see the drgn-343 and armr-343
spoilers).  That's partly because there are a couple of good
opportunities to get reflection fairly early in the game, as you've
discovered.  Also, at least in my experience, magic resistance is much
more important than reflection in pretty much all stages of the game.
You can easily ascend without reflection; ascending without magic
resistance would be an impressive accomplishment.

But, there's no need to beat yourself up (as always, it's best to let
the monsters do that).  In the early game, *any* dragon scale mail is
a fantastic deal, simply because it provides a ton of AC protection in
a really lightweight item.  Plus, the nice thing about SDSM is that it
complements the non-suit source of MR well and still leaves the amulet
slot free for life saving and both hands free for two-weaponing or
spell casting.

> Oh well. Noob question now: is it worth it for me to wear both?

Reflection is yes or no.  If you have it from one item, then you have
it, and additional items don't provide "more" reflection.  But keep
the amulet, obviously, either in your bag or in a stash.  If you run
across the "other" DSM, you may decide to switch.

One poster mentioned that it's useful to wear the amulet for a
redundant source.  That's because, as you've undoubtedly already
discovered, there are several ways that monsters can relieve you of
protection provided by worn or carried items, either for just a couple
of turns, for a few dozen turns (until you hunt them down and kill
them), or even permanently.

Personally, I'd avoid the extra food consumption and just keep the
amulet in my bag.  In fact, for me, it would be a low-priority item
for purposes of inventory management and would probably end up in a
cache somewhere.  But that's just me...

